            <ArticleTitle>[Parvovirus B19 atypical acrosyndrome].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>254-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Parvovirus B19 infection is a very polymorphic eruption. We describe a new clinical form.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 4-year-old child was hospitalized for a painful acrocyanosis localised at the feet and the tip of the nose, which disappeared without any treatment in three weeks. The screening revealed a parvovirus B19 infection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This eruption differs from the classic &quot;gloves and socks syndrome&quot; because of the presence of plantar and nasal painful erythrocyanosis and the young age of the child.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
